          "Jesus regularly refused requests. He left crowds who wanted him to stay (Mark 1:35-38). He would not let his family pull him away from his mission (Mark 3:31-35). He refused to be made king on the crowd’s terms (John 6:15). He told Peter, “Get behind me, Satan!” when Peter tried to turn him from the cross (Matt 16:23). None of this was ego. It was faithfulness to the Father and to his calling.",
          "He also confronted unfair treatment. When struck at his trial, he did not retaliate, but he did not pretend it was right either: in effect, “If I have spoken evil, show the evil; but if well, why do you strike me?” (John 18:23, paraphrase). He named injustice calmly, without hatred and without self-erasure. He could also stay silent when silence served his purpose (Matt 27:12-14). His choices were not driven by the need to look strong or the fear of looking weak.",
          "Jesus’ self-possession shows up in his words about his life: “No one takes it away from me, but I lay it down by myself” (John 10:18). Even his self-giving was free, not extracted. That is the difference between a gift and being used. Love gives freely; it does not simply let itself be taken.",
          "So confronting or refusing is not automatically ego. It can be pride, but it can also be the humble, honest, loving act of a person who knows they are loved by God and wants the same for others. Ask the questions above, stay soft in heart and clear in word, and trust that the God who made you with dignity is pleased when you stop pretending you have none."
